WEBVTT

1
00:00:10.868 --> 00:00:12.775
Welcome back to part 2.

2
00:00:13.282 --> 00:00:16.437
In part 1 we covered Reflection of Light by

3
00:00:16.437 --> 00:00:20.151
Spherical Mirrors, Sign Convention, Refraction and Snell's Law and

4
00:00:20.151 --> 00:00:23.201
Total Internal Reflection and the Critical Angle.

5
00:00:23.735 --> 00:00:25.937
Now we continue with Power of a Lens.

6
00:00:27.976 --> 00:00:30.800
We have completed Refraction by a Lens.

7
00:00:31.319 --> 00:00:33.595
Now we start Power of a Lens.

8
00:00:34.116 --> 00:00:37.726
Have you ever wondered how strong a magnifying glass is?

9
00:00:38.259 --> 00:00:40.438
The power P of a lens is defined as

10
00:00:40.438 --> 00:00:43.224
the reciprocal of its focal length f in metres,

11
00:00:43.224 --> 00:00:45.714
given by P = 1 / f.

12
00:00:46.236 --> 00:00:48.155
It is measured in dioptre.

13
00:00:48.688 --> 00:00:50.652
It is positive for a convex lens

14
00:00:50.652 --> 00:00:52.628
and negative for a concave lens.

15
00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:58.005
For thin lenses in contact, 1 / f equals 1 /

16
00:00:58.005 --> 00:01:01.803
f1 plus 1 / f2, so P equals P1 plus P2.

17
00:01:01.803 --> 00:01:05.967
Let us find the combined power and focal length of a

18
00:01:05.967 --> 00:01:09.443
5 dioptre lens in contact with a minus 2 dioptre lens.

19
00:01:09.443 --> 00:01:14.312
Adding them gives P = 5 - 2 = 3 dioptre.

20
00:01:14.312 --> 00:01:20.622
The focal length is f = 1 / 3 = 0.33 m.

21
00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:23.916
Check yourself.

22
00:01:24.435 --> 00:01:27.554
Why is the power of a convex lens considered positive

23
00:01:27.554 --> 00:01:30.852
while the power of a concave lens is considered negative?

24
00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:41.398
The power of a lens is the reciprocal of its focal length.

25
00:01:41.917 --> 00:01:45.143
Because a convex lens has a positive focal length,

26
00:01:45.143 --> 00:01:48.298
its power is positive, whereas a concave lens has

27
00:01:48.298 --> 00:01:51.298
a negative focal length, making its power negative.

28
00:01:53.336 --> 00:01:55.779
We have completed Power of a Lens.

29
00:01:56.298 --> 00:01:59.229
Now we start Refraction through a Prism.

30
00:01:59.750 --> 00:02:01.786
Have you ever wondered how a prism

31
00:02:01.786 --> 00:02:03.884
splits white light into a rainbow?

32
00:02:04.405 --> 00:02:07.979
Let us examine refraction through a transparent prism.

33
00:02:08.488 --> 00:02:11.488
Consider a ray of light incident on the first face of the

34
00:02:11.488 --> 00:02:16.048
prism at angle i, refracting at angle r1, striking the second face

35
00:02:16.048 --> 00:02:19.693
at angle r2, and emerging into the air at angle e.

36
00:02:20.226 --> 00:02:22.465
The angle between the incident ray and the

37
00:02:22.465 --> 00:02:25.336
emergent ray is the angle of deviation delta.

38
00:02:25.857 --> 00:02:28.905
Geometry shows that the total angle of deviation is

39
00:02:28.905 --> 00:02:32.060
given by delta = i + e - A,

40
00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:34.024
where A is the angle of the prism.

41
00:02:36.028 --> 00:02:40.076
As the angle of incidence increases, the angle of deviation first

42
00:02:40.076 --> 00:02:45.042
decreases, reaches a unique minimum value Dm, and then increases.

43
00:02:45.549 --> 00:02:49.430
At this position of minimum deviation, the ray passes symmetrically

44
00:02:49.430 --> 00:02:53.133
through the prism, meaning the angle of incidence equals the

45
00:02:53.133 --> 00:02:56.645
angle of emergence, so i = e, and the internal

46
00:02:56.645 --> 00:03:01.680
angles satisfy r1 = r2 = A / 2.

47
00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:06.871
Substituting these conditions into Snell's law yields the prism formula n =

48
00:03:06.871 --> 00:03:10.811
sin((A + Dm) / 2) / sin(A / 2), which allows us

49
00:03:10.811 --> 00:03:14.609
to experimentally determine the refractive index n of the prism material.

50
00:03:17.232 --> 00:03:20.246
We have completed Refraction through a Prism.

51
00:03:20.777 --> 00:03:23.565
Now we start Dispersion by a Prism.

52
00:03:24.099 --> 00:03:27.349
Have you ever wondered how a simple glass prism splits a

53
00:03:27.349 --> 00:03:30.577
beam of white light into a stunning rainbow of colours?

54
00:03:31.122 --> 00:03:34.161
This phenomenon is called dispersion of light.

55
00:03:34.682 --> 00:03:38.289
When white light passes through a prism, violet light is bent

56
00:03:38.289 --> 00:03:41.325
the most while red light is bent the least because the

57
00:03:41.325 --> 00:03:45.196
refractive index of glass depends on the wavelength of light.

58
00:03:45.718 --> 00:03:49.765
Since each colour has a different wavelength, it travels at a slightly

59
00:03:49.765 --> 00:03:53.801
different speed inside the glass and thus deviates by a unique angle.

60
00:03:55.820 --> 00:04:00.582
Let us examine Table 9.2 from your textbook, which lists refractive

61
00:04:00.582 --> 00:04:04.930
indices at specific wavelengths for crown glass and flint glass.

62
00:04:05.449 --> 00:04:17.020
At wavelengths of 396.9, 486.1, 589.3, and 656.3 nanometre, crown glass

63
00:04:17.020 --> 00:04:26.987
has refractive indices of 1.533, 1.523, 1.517, and 1.515 respectively.

64
00:04:27.508 --> 00:04:29.996
For flint glass at the same wavelengths, the

65
00:04:29.996 --> 00:04:38.403
refractive indices are 1.663, 1.639, 1.627, and 1.622.

66
00:04:38.925 --> 00:04:42.377
Notice how the refractive index decreases as the wavelength

67
00:04:42.377 --> 00:04:44.996
increases from the violet end to the red end

68
00:04:44.996 --> 00:04:47.675
of the visible spectrum for both glass types.

69
00:04:49.696 --> 00:04:54.008
Why do thick lenses suffer from a defect known as chromatic aberration?

70
00:04:54.527 --> 00:04:57.801
Because a thick lens acts like a combination of prisms

71
00:04:57.801 --> 00:05:02.625
stacked together, refracting different wavelengths of light by varying amounts.

72
00:05:03.146 --> 00:05:05.920
When white light passes through a thick lens, blue

73
00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:08.622
light focuses closer to the lens while red light

74
00:05:08.622 --> 00:05:13.113
focuses further away, producing blurred images with coloured fringes.

75
00:05:13.634 --> 00:05:16.658
This dispersion effect limits the optical clarity

76
00:05:16.658 --> 00:05:20.075
of simple uncorrected lenses in precision instruments.

77
00:05:22.140 --> 00:05:23.416
Check yourself.

78
00:05:23.935 --> 00:05:26.161
Why does violet light bend more than

79
00:05:26.161 --> 00:05:28.269
red light when passing through a prism?

80
00:05:35.296 --> 00:05:38.915
Because the refractive index of glass depends on the wavelength of

81
00:05:38.915 --> 00:05:42.927
light, causing shorter wavelengths like violet to travel at a different

82
00:05:42.927 --> 00:05:46.129
speed and deviate more than longer wavelengths like red.

83
00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:51.159
We have completed Dispersion by a Prism.

84
00:05:51.678 --> 00:05:55.763
Now we start Natural Phenomena due to Sunlight: The Rainbow.

85
00:05:56.285 --> 00:05:58.737
Have you ever wondered how a brilliant arc of

86
00:05:58.737 --> 00:06:01.728
colours appears in the sky after a rain shower?

87
00:06:02.249 --> 00:06:05.344
A rainbow is a wonderful natural phenomenon caused by

88
00:06:05.344 --> 00:06:09.118
the combined effect of dispersion, refraction, and reflection of

89
00:06:09.118 --> 00:06:13.037
sunlight by spherical raindrops suspended in the atmosphere.

90
00:06:13.570 --> 00:06:17.428
For a rainbow to be visible to an observer, two essential conditions

91
00:06:17.428 --> 00:06:21.368
must be met: the Sun must be positioned behind the observer, and

92
00:06:21.368 --> 00:06:25.204
there must be falling or suspended rain in front of the observer.

93
00:06:25.737 --> 00:06:30.213
Sunlight enters a spherical raindrop, undergoes refraction at the front

94
00:06:30.213 --> 00:06:34.880
surface, is dispersed into its constituent colours, reflects internally from

95
00:06:34.880 --> 00:06:38.320
the back surface of the drop, and finally refracts again

96
00:06:38.320 --> 00:06:41.190
as it emerges into the air towards the observer.

97
00:06:43.232 --> 00:06:45.696
The primary rainbow is formed by rays that

98
00:06:45.696 --> 00:06:49.770
undergo exactly one internal reflection inside the raindrop.

99
00:06:50.289 --> 00:06:53.753
The resulting bow shows red on the outer edge, at an angle

100
00:06:53.753 --> 00:06:58.661
of 42 degrees, and violet on the inner edge, at 40 degrees.

101
00:06:59.182 --> 00:07:02.027
Sometimes, a second, fainter bow called the

102
00:07:02.027 --> 00:07:05.518
secondary rainbow appears outside the primary one.

103
00:07:06.039 --> 00:07:08.468
The secondary rainbow is produced by rays

104
00:07:08.468 --> 00:07:12.041
undergoing two internal reflections inside the raindrops.

105
00:07:12.563 --> 00:07:16.861
Because of the extra reflection, the intensity is significantly lower

106
00:07:16.861 --> 00:07:20.289
and the order of colours is completely reversed, appearing with

107
00:07:20.289 --> 00:07:23.682
violet on the outer edge at 53 degrees and red

108
00:07:23.682 --> 00:07:25.789
on the inner edge at 50 degrees.

109
00:07:27.820 --> 00:07:29.096
Check yourself.

110
00:07:29.615 --> 00:07:32.389
What optical phenomena combined with sunlight and

111
00:07:32.389 --> 00:07:34.758
raindrops cause the formation of a rainbow?

112
00:07:41.792 --> 00:07:45.161
A rainbow is caused by the combined effect of refraction,

113
00:07:45.161 --> 00:07:49.435
dispersion, and internal reflection of sunlight by spherical raindrops.

114
00:07:51.484 --> 00:07:55.760
We have completed Natural Phenomena due to Sunlight: The Rainbow.

115
00:07:56.279 --> 00:07:58.603
Now we start Scattering of Light.

116
00:07:59.124 --> 00:08:01.136
Have you ever wondered why the sky

117
00:08:01.136 --> 00:08:03.567
above us appears blue during the day?

118
00:08:04.101 --> 00:08:08.232
Scattering is the phenomenon where light is deflected in various directions

119
00:08:08.232 --> 00:08:13.032
by particles in its path, such as atmospheric molecules and dust.

120
00:08:13.553 --> 00:08:16.434
When the particles are much smaller than the wavelength,

121
00:08:16.434 --> 00:08:20.434
Rayleigh scattering applies, and the scattered intensity is proportional

122
00:08:20.434 --> 00:08:23.103
to one over wavelength to the fourth power.

123
00:08:23.624 --> 00:08:27.291
So shorter wavelengths scatter much more than longer ones,

124
00:08:27.291 --> 00:08:30.184
which explains why the blue sky is so prominent.

125
00:08:32.208 --> 00:08:33.898
Why does the sun look red at

126
00:08:33.898 --> 00:08:37.056
sunrise and sunset while clouds look white?

127
00:08:37.598 --> 00:08:42.039
At sunset, light travels through a thicker atmosphere layer, scattering

128
00:08:42.039 --> 00:08:45.994
away shorter blue wavelengths and leaving the longer red wavelengths.

129
00:08:46.515 --> 00:08:49.765
Meanwhile, clouds look white because water droplets

130
00:08:49.765 --> 00:08:52.670
scatter all wavelengths of white light equally.

131
00:08:54.716 --> 00:08:55.992
Check yourself.

132
00:08:56.511 --> 00:08:58.964
According to Rayleigh scattering, why does the

133
00:08:58.964 --> 00:09:00.726
sky appear blue during the day?

134
00:09:07.752 --> 00:09:11.550
Shorter wavelengths of light scatter much more than longer wavelengths

135
00:09:11.550 --> 00:09:15.895
when deflected by atmospheric particles, making the blue color prominent.

136
00:09:17.924 --> 00:09:19.734
We have completed Scattering of Light.

137
00:09:19.734 --> 00:09:23.553
Now we start Optical Instruments: The Eye.

138
00:09:23.553 --> 00:09:26.684
Have you ever wondered how the human eye

139
00:09:26.684 --> 00:09:29.731
automatically focuses on objects both near and far?

140
00:09:29.731 --> 00:09:34.291
Light enters the eye through the transparent cornea and passes through

141
00:09:34.291 --> 00:09:37.993
the pupil, an aperture regulated in size by the pigmented iris.

142
00:09:37.993 --> 00:09:43.362
Behind the pupil, the crystalline eye lens refracts the light rays to

143
00:09:43.362 --> 00:09:47.541
form a sharp, inverted real image on the sensitive retina, which converts

144
00:09:47.541 --> 00:09:51.648
the optical signal into electrical nerve impulses sent to the brain.

145
00:09:51.648 --> 00:09:55.076
The near point of a normal human eye is situated

146
00:09:55.076 --> 00:09:59.648
at a standard distance of 25 cm, closer than which

147
00:09:59.648 --> 00:10:03.231
objects cannot be seen clearly without strain, while the far

148
00:10:03.231 --> 00:10:06.255
point lies at infinity for a relaxed normal eye.

149
00:10:08.296 --> 00:10:11.906
How does the eye adjust its focus for different distances?

150
00:10:12.436 --> 00:10:17.067
Through the process of accommodation, ciliary muscles contract or relax to

151
00:10:17.067 --> 00:10:21.010
alter the curvature and focal length of the flexible eye lens.

152
00:10:21.532 --> 00:10:24.570
However, structural defects often occur.

153
00:10:25.103 --> 00:10:30.544
Myopia, or nearsightedness, is a condition where distant objects appear blurred because

154
00:10:30.544 --> 00:10:34.139
the image forms in front of the retina; it is corrected using

155
00:10:34.139 --> 00:10:38.665
a concave lens of suitable focal length that diverges the incoming rays.

156
00:10:39.186 --> 00:10:45.627
Conversely, hypermetropia, or farsightedness, occurs when nearby images form behind the retina,

157
00:10:45.627 --> 00:10:49.865
which is corrected using a converging convex lens that adds the necessary

158
00:10:49.865 --> 00:10:53.079
refraction to pull the focal point back onto the retina.

159
00:10:55.116 --> 00:10:58.440
We have completed Optical Instruments: The Eye.

160
00:10:58.971 --> 00:11:01.128
Now we start The Microscope.

161
00:11:01.649 --> 00:11:05.592
Have you ever tried reading tiny text with a magnifying glass?

162
00:11:06.126 --> 00:11:08.923
A simple microscope is a convex lens used

163
00:11:08.923 --> 00:11:12.307
as a magnifier to view small objects clearly.

164
00:11:12.911 --> 00:11:16.185
When the final virtual image is formed at the near point,

165
00:11:16.185 --> 00:11:19.721
the magnifying power is given by m equals 1 plus D

166
00:11:19.721 --> 00:11:23.149
by f, where D is the least distance of distinct vision

167
00:11:23.149 --> 00:11:25.756
and f is the focal length of the convex lens.

168
00:11:27.776 --> 00:11:30.169
To achieve much higher magnification than a

169
00:11:30.169 --> 00:11:33.362
simple lens, we use a compound microscope.

170
00:11:33.881 --> 00:11:39.024
The objective lens forms a real, inverted, and magnified intermediate image,

171
00:11:39.024 --> 00:11:43.383
which the eyepiece then further magnifies as a virtual final image.

172
00:11:43.905 --> 00:11:48.238
The total magnifying power is the product of the objective magnification

173
00:11:48.238 --> 00:11:52.857
and the eyepiece magnification, given approximately by m equals L by

174
00:11:52.857 --> 00:11:56.452
f_o times D by f_e, where L is the tube length.

175
00:11:58.492 --> 00:11:59.768
Check yourself.

176
00:12:00.287 --> 00:12:04.216
In a compound microscope, what type of intermediate image is formed

177
00:12:04.216 --> 00:12:07.716
by the objective lens, and how does the eyepiece treat it?

178
00:12:14.744 --> 00:12:20.089
The objective lens forms a real, inverted, and magnified intermediate image, which

179
00:12:20.089 --> 00:12:24.220
the eyepiece then further magnifies to produce a virtual final image.

180
00:12:26.260 --> 00:12:28.608
We have completed The Microscope.

181
00:12:29.127 --> 00:12:31.153
Now we start The Telescope.

182
00:12:31.674 --> 00:12:34.781
Have you ever wondered how astronomers gaze deep into

183
00:12:34.781 --> 00:12:37.689
the night sky to view distant stars and planets?

184
00:12:38.210 --> 00:12:42.317
An astronomical refracting telescope consists of an objective lens of

185
00:12:42.317 --> 00:12:45.674
large focal length and a large aperture to gather maximum

186
00:12:45.674 --> 00:12:49.341
light from distant celestial objects, and an eyepiece of small

187
00:12:49.341 --> 00:12:52.022
focal length to magnify the formed image.

188
00:12:52.543 --> 00:12:56.665
In normal adjustment, the final image is formed at infinity.

189
00:12:57.210 --> 00:13:00.103
The magnifying power m is given by the ratio of

190
00:13:00.103 --> 00:13:03.103
focal length of the objective f_o to focal length of

191
00:13:03.103 --> 00:13:07.186
the eyepiece f_e, so that m equals f_o by f_e,

192
00:13:07.186 --> 00:13:10.150
and the tube length equals f_o plus f_e.

193
00:13:12.216 --> 00:13:17.680
While refracting telescopes are useful, large lenses suffer from chromatic aberration and

194
00:13:17.680 --> 00:13:21.337
are heavy, so they are difficult to support and can sag.

195
00:13:21.856 --> 00:13:27.249
To overcome this, the reflecting telescope uses a parabolic mirror objective, which

196
00:13:27.249 --> 00:13:32.526
avoids chromatic aberration and permits a large aperture to collect faint starlight.

197
00:13:33.047 --> 00:13:37.916
The Cassegrain arrangement employs a large parabolic primary mirror with a central

198
00:13:37.916 --> 00:13:41.702
hole and a convex secondary mirror that reflects light back through the

199
00:13:41.702 --> 00:13:46.321
hole to the eyepiece, creating a compact system with reduced spherical and

200
00:13:46.321 --> 00:13:50.095
chromatic aberration, though coma can still occur off the axis.

201
00:13:52.124 --> 00:13:53.400
Check yourself.

202
00:13:53.919 --> 00:13:56.717
Why is a reflecting telescope preferred over a

203
00:13:56.717 --> 00:14:00.467
refracting telescope when observing faint celestial objects?

204
00:14:07.488 --> 00:14:12.928
A reflecting telescope uses a mirror objective that completely avoids chromatic aberration

205
00:14:12.928 --> 00:14:16.786
and allows for a much larger aperture to collect fainter starlight.

206
00:14:18.836 --> 00:14:21.088
We have completed The Telescope.

207
00:14:21.607 --> 00:14:24.646
Now we start Summary and Points to Remember.

208
00:14:25.191 --> 00:14:28.527
Let us summarize the main results of ray optics.

209
00:14:29.048 --> 00:14:31.822
Light travels in straight lines and satisfies

210
00:14:31.822 --> 00:14:34.300
the laws of reflection and refraction.

211
00:14:34.834 --> 00:14:37.881
Spherical mirrors obey the mirror equation 1 / v

212
00:14:37.881 --> 00:14:40.726
+ 1 / u = 1 / f, with

213
00:14:40.726 --> 00:14:43.086
focal length f = R / 2.

214
00:14:43.619 --> 00:14:47.976
Refraction across an interface follows Snell's law n_21 = sin

215
00:14:47.976 --> 00:14:51.536
i / sin r, and total internal reflection occurs for

216
00:14:51.536 --> 00:14:55.931
angles exceeding the critical angle sin i_c = n_21.

217
00:14:56.465 --> 00:14:59.667
Lenses use refraction to form images, governed by

218
00:14:59.667 --> 00:15:02.681
the lens maker's formula and lens formula.

219
00:15:03.215 --> 00:15:06.455
Prisms produce deviation and dispersion of light.

220
00:15:07.012 --> 00:15:12.895
Optical instruments like microscopes and telescopes enhance vision and angular magnification.

221
00:15:13.429 --> 00:15:16.250
Thank you for joining this lesson on ray optics.

